---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The comprehensive MaxBars reference — FlatBars' flagship surface: most of FullBars,
// reinterpreted, plus infix operators, a pipe, and bare loop variables. Structured deltas-first
// (operators → pipes → the identity → loop vars), Jinja-style around an
// expression core rather than re-walking the Handlebars spine — then the inherited
// FullBars constructs (shown only with operators woven through, linking to
// /fullbars rather than re-teaching), the divergences, compile, and a "when (not)
// to use" close. Prose is illustrative only; every runnable example comes from the
// single gate-validated source ../maxbars.mjs and renders through the real
// `maxbars` engine. The ~80-helper prelude is NOT re-listed (the generated catalog
// owns that) — this page teaches the expression layer and links the catalog.
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as ex } from "../maxbars.mjs";

const SPEC_BASE = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SPEC_BASE || "/flatbars/";

// Sticky left-nav: MaxBars concepts, then the sibling surfaces, then the Lab.
const groups = [
  {
    label: "MaxBars",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What MaxBars is" },
      { href: "#operators", label: "Operators" },
      { href: "#pipes", label: "Pipes" },
      { href: "#identity", label: "The identity" },
      { href: "#loops", label: "Loop variables" },
      { href: "#inherited", label: "From FullBars" },
      { href: "#divergences", label: "Where it diverges" },
      { href: "#compile", label: "Compiles to JS" },
      { href: "#when", label: "When (not) to use" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Other surfaces",
    items: [
      { href: "/minbars", label: "MinBars — Mustache" },
      { href: "/rawbars", label: "RawBars — the core" },
      { href: "/fullbars", label: "FullBars — Handlebars" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];

// Inline tag references — kept in a helper so Astro never parses the {{ }}.
const t = (s) => s;
---

<Reference
  title="MaxBars — the FlatBars expression language"
  subtitle="MaxBars is FlatBars' flagship surface — an expression layer over the same meaning-free core: infix operators ({{ a >= b }}), a pipe ({{ value | helper }}), and bare loop variables. The most capable surface in the family; it borrows most of FullBars and adds the expression freedom FullBars holds back — and underneath, every operator is still just a helper application."
  groups={groups}
  spec="maxbars"
  dialect="maxbars"
>
  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What MaxBars is</h2>
    <p>
      <b>MaxBars is FlatBars' flagship surface — an expression layer over the same
      meaning-free core.</b> If you've read the <a href="/fullbars">FullBars reference</a>,
      you'll recognise most of it: MaxBars borrows the bulk of FullBars and adds three
      things on top (a few FullBars-only constructs are the deliberate exceptions —
      see <a href="#divergences">where MaxBars and FullBars diverge</a>):
    </p>
    <ul class="conv">
      <li><b>Infix operators</b> — <code>{t("{{score >= 50}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{price * qty}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{a && b}}")}</code> — instead of nesting subexpressions.</li>
      <li><b>A pipe</b> — <code>{t("{{value | helper}}")}</code> — to read transformations left-to-right.</li>
      <li><b>Bare loop variables</b> — <code>{t("index1")}</code>, <code>{t("first")}</code>, <code>{t("length")}</code> — inside <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of it is new machinery. <code>{t("{{price * qty}}")}</code> <em>desugars to</em>
      <code>{t("(multiply price qty)")}</code> — the same helper application FullBars and
      RawBars run. MaxBars is sugar that compiles down the ladder, not a second engine.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.intro.template} data={ex.intro.data} />
    <div class="callout info">
      <p>
        <b>Where MaxBars sits.</b> It's the most permissive surface in the family —
        expressions live in the template. If you want strict model-view separation
        (the StringTemplate philosophy — no logic in templates), drop down the ladder:
        <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> is the same engine with none of this sugar, and
        <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a> sits in between. One core, four levels of
        expressiveness.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────── Operators ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="operators">
    <h2>Operators</h2>
    <p>
      Every operator is infix sugar for a prelude helper. Arithmetic is <b>strictly
      numeric</b> — there is no string <code>+</code> (that throws). Comparisons are
      non-associative (no <code>{t("a < b < c")}</code> chains). Group with parentheses
      to control precedence.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Operators</th><th>Desugars to</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>+ - * / %</code></td><td><code>add subtract multiply divide modulo</code></td><td><code>{t("{{price * qty}}")}</code> → <code>12</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>== != &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</code></td><td><code>eq ne lt gt lte gte</code></td><td><code>{t("{{qty >= 1}}")}</code> → <code>true</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&amp;&amp; || !</code></td><td><code>and or not</code></td><td><code>{t("{{a && !b}}")}</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>??</code></td><td><code>coalesce</code></td><td><code>{t("{{nick ?? name}}")}</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Comparisons in conditions</h3>
    <p>
      The everyday use: a comparison as the head of <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code>. Where
      FullBars writes the subexpression <code>{t("{{#if (gte qty 1)}}")}</code>, MaxBars
      writes the infix form.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.comparison.template} data={ex.comparison.data} />
    <h3>Arithmetic</h3>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.math.template} data={ex.math.data} />
    <h3>Logic and <code>??</code></h3>
    <p>
      <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds tighter than <code>||</code> and looser than the
      comparisons; parenthesise when in doubt. <code>??</code> is <b>null-coalescing</b>,
      not a truthiness fallback — it only substitutes when the left side is null/undefined,
      so an empty string or <code>0</code> on the left is kept.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.logic.template} data={ex.logic.data} />
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.coalesce.template} data={ex.coalesce.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────── Pipes ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="pipes">
    <h2>Pipes</h2>
    <p>
      The pipe <code>|</code> feeds the value on its left in as the helper's
      <b>first argument</b>: <code>{t("{{name | uppercase}}")}</code> is exactly
      <code>{t("(uppercase name)")}</code>. Any further arguments follow the helper
      name, so <code>{t("{{price | toFixed 2}}")}</code> is <code>{t("(toFixed price 2)")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.pipeSimple.template} data={ex.pipeSimple.data} />
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.pipeArgs.template} data={ex.pipeArgs.data} />
    <h3>Chaining</h3>
    <p>
      Pipes chain left-to-right, each stage feeding the next — the readable way to
      express a sequence of transforms that would otherwise nest inside-out. Read this
      as "pull the names out of the items, then join them":
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.pipeChain.template} data={ex.pipeChain.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The right-hand side of a pipe is a helper from the
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}prelude.html`}>prelude catalog</a> (here <code>pluck</code>,
      <code>join</code>, <code>uppercase</code>, <code>toFixed</code>) — or one a host
      registers. The pipe doesn't add helpers; it's a second way to call them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────── The identity: op ⇄ helper ⇄ subexpr ────────────── -->
  <section id="identity">
    <h2>The identity: operator ⇄ helper ⇄ subexpression</h2>
    <p>
      This is the idea the whole family rests on. An infix operator, a named helper
      call, and a parenthesised subexpression are <b>the same operation written three
      ways</b> — they parse to the identical core application and render identically.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.identity.template} data={ex.identity.data} />
    <p>
      So "MaxBars vs FullBars vs RawBars" isn't three engines — it's how much sugar the
      surface gives you over one core. <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> shows the bottom of
      that ladder, where <code>{t("(multiply price qty)")}</code> is all there is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────── Loop variables ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="loops">
    <h2>Loop variables</h2>
    <p>
      Inside <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>, MaxBars exposes the loop state as
      <b>bare variables</b> — no sigil, just names you apply like any other helper.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>{t("index0")}</code> / <code>{t("index1")}</code></td><td>position, 0-based / 1-based</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>{t("rindex0")}</code> / <code>{t("rindex1")}</code></td><td>remaining, 0-based / 1-based</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>{t("first")}</code> / <code>{t("last")}</code></td><td><code>true</code> on the first / last element</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>{t("length")}</code></td><td>the collection's length</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>{t("key")}</code></td><td>the property name when iterating an object</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.loopVars.template} data={ex.loopVars.data} />
    <h3>Over an object</h3>
    <p>Iterating an object, <code>{t("key")}</code> is the property name and <code>{t("{{this}}")}</code> the value.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.loopObject.template} data={ex.loopObject.data} />
    <h3>Block parameters</h3>
    <p>
      A trailing <code>{t("as |name …|")}</code> binds scoped names over the block body — exactly as
      <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>. They stay in scope inside nested blocks, so an inner loop can
      still reach an outer element. The bars are the block-parameter delimiter, not the pipe operator;
      to <em>pipe</em> a head argument, parenthesise it — <code>{t("{{#each (rows | reverse) as |r|}}")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.blockParams.template} data={ex.blockParams.data} />
    <h3>Labelled loops</h3>
    <p>
      Block params reach an outer <em>element</em>, but not the outer loop's <code>{t("index")}</code> /
      <code>{t("length")}</code> / <code>{t("first")}</code>. A trailing <code>{t("label NAME")}</code> clause
      (ADR-013) names the loop <b>frame</b>, binding it as an
      object whose fields are the bare loop variables — so an inner loop reads
      <code>{t("outer.index1")}</code>, <code>{t("outer.length")}</code>, <code>{t("outer.first")}</code> of the
      enclosing loop. It is an immutable per-iteration snapshot, reached by ordinary path lookup.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.labelledLoop.template} data={ex.labelledLoop.data} />
    <h3>The context model: <code>parent</code> / <code>root</code></h3>
    <p>
      Where <code>{t("loop")}</code> / <code>{t("outer")}</code> read loop <em>metadata</em>,
      <code>{t("parent")}</code> reads the enclosing <em>context</em> and <code>{t("root")}</code> the root —
      both chainable: <code>{t("parent.parent.x")}</code> climbs two contexts, and <code>{t("loop.parent")}</code> is
      the enclosing loop. There is no <code>{t("@")}</code> and no <code>{t("../")}</code> in MaxBars
      (<code>{t("@root")}</code> → <code>{t("root")}</code>, <code>{t("../x")}</code> → <code>{t("parent.x")}</code>); an
      <code>{t("@")}</code>-name is a parse error.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.contextModel.template} data={ex.contextModel.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Inherited from FullBars ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="inherited">
    <h2>Most of FullBars — with operators inside</h2>
    <p>
      MaxBars borrows FullBars's core underneath, so paths (<code>{t("user.address.city")}</code>,
      <code>{t("../x")}</code>, <code>{t("@root")}</code>), the block helpers
      <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{#with}}")}</code> /
      <code>{t("{{#unless}}")}</code>, escaping, and subexpressions all work as the
      <a href="/fullbars">FullBars reference</a> documents them. The headline addition is that
      operators and pipes can now appear anywhere a value can — and a handful of FullBars-only
      constructs are the deliberate exceptions (see <a href="#divergences">where it diverges</a>).
      Here a single
      <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> mixes a path, arithmetic, a pipe, and an <code>==</code>:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.inheritedEach.template} data={ex.inheritedEach.data} />
    <h3><code>with</code> and subexpressions</h3>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.withBlock.template} data={ex.withBlock.data} />
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.subexpr.template} data={ex.subexpr.data} />
    <h3>Escaping</h3>
    <p><code>{t("{{x}}")}</code> HTML-escapes; the triple-stash <code>{t("{{{x}}}")}</code> emits raw markup — unchanged from FullBars.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.escaping.template} data={ex.escaping.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────── Where MaxBars diverges ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="divergences">
    <h2>Where MaxBars diverges from FullBars</h2>
    <p>
      The expression layer costs a few things FullBars has. Each is a direct
      consequence of putting operators and a pipe into the grammar — none is a
      surprise once you see why.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>FullBars</th><th>MaxBars</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Pipe in a block head <code>{t("{{#each xs | f}}")}</code></td>
          <td>must be <b>parenthesised</b></td>
          <td>The head grammar drops the top-level pipe rung so a bar can delimit <code>{t("as |x|")}</code>; write <code>{t("{{#each (xs | f) as |x|}}")}</code>. An unparenthesised head bar is treated as structure (like FullBars), not a pipe.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Block partials + <code>{t("{{yield}}")}</code></td>
          <td><b>inline only</b></td>
          <td>Inline-defined partials work — <code>{t("{{#inline \"layout\"}}<{{yield}}>{{/inline}}")}</code> then <code>{t("{{#partial \"layout\"}}…{{/partial}}")}</code> — where <code>{t("{{yield}}")}</code> renders the caller's block body (MaxBars' bare spelling of Handlebars' <code>{t("{{> @partial-block}}")}</code>; the <code>@</code> sigil is reserved, so that Handlebars form is rejected). Not supported: the <code>{t("{{#*inline}}")}</code> <em>decorator</em> (FullBars-only; MaxBars uses bare <code>{t("{{#inline}}")}</code>) and host-threaded <em>external</em> partial files — for those use <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Infix anywhere</td>
          <td>not in a <b>clause separator</b></td>
          <td><code>{t("{{else if (gt n 0)}}")}</code> must parenthesise. The bare form <code>{t("{{else if n > 0}}")}</code> parses but silently takes the wrong branch.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>JS-flavoured <code>+</code></td>
          <td>arithmetic is <b>strictly numeric</b></td>
          <td><code>{t("\"a\" + \"b\"")}</code> throws — there is no string concatenation operator.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mustache-style <code>{t("{{^x}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{&x}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{{{raw}}}}")}</code></td>
          <td>rejected</td>
          <td>MaxBars sets <code>extras = false</code> — it's not the Handlebars-compatibility dialect.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="tip">
      The separator rule is the one real trap: a bare infix condition in
      <code>{t("{{else if …}}")}</code> doesn't error, it just renders wrong. Always
      wrap it. Here's the correct, parenthesised form:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.separatorParens.template} data={ex.separatorParens.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────── Compiles to JS ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="compile">
    <h2>Compiles to JS</h2>
    <p>
      Like the other surfaces, MaxBars <b>compiles to a standalone JS module</b>, and
      FlatBars gates that the compiled module and the interpreter produce
      <b>byte-identical</b> output — operators and pipes included, since they desugar
      before compilation. Expand <b>Compiled JS</b> on the loop example:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={ex.loopVars.template} data={ex.loopVars.data} compile />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────── When (not) to use MaxBars ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="when">
    <h2>When (not) to use MaxBars</h2>
    <p>
      MaxBars is the right surface when the <b>view genuinely needs expressions</b> —
      formatting, thresholds, small computed values — and you'd rather write
      <code>{t("{{price * qty | toFixed 2}}")}</code> than thread a pre-computed field
      through your model for every cell.
    </p>
    <p>Reach for a sibling instead when:</p>
    <ul class="conv">
      <li>You want <b>strict model-view separation</b> — push all logic into the model and keep templates dumb. That's <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> (or, outside this family, StringTemplate).</li>
      <li>You want <b>Handlebars compatibility</b> — block params, partials, the <code>{t("{{&x}}")}</code> spellings. That's <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>.</li>
      <li>You're porting <b>Mustache</b> templates. That's <a href="/minbars">MinBars</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Same engine, same compiler, same ~80-helper <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}prelude.html`}>prelude</a>
      under all four — MaxBars just turns the most sugar on. Jump into the
      <a href="/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars">Lab</a> and start typing, or read the
      normative <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}maxbars.html`}>maxbars.adoc</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Reference>
